{"entries":[{"id":"tsk-jhn-12-1","title":"John 12:1","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":12,"verseStart":1,"chapterEnd":12,"verseEnd":1},"content":"<p>1 Jesus excuses Mary anointing his feet. 9 The people flock to see Lazarus. 10 The chief priests consult to kill him. 12 Christ rides into Jerusalem. 20 Greeks desire to see Jesus. 23 He foretells his death. 37 The Jews are generally blinded; 42 yet many chief rulers believe, but do not confess him; 44 therefore Jesus calls earnestly for confession of faith.</p>\n<p>six. 11:55 Bethany. 11:1,44; Mt 21:17; Mr 11:11; Lu 24:50</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-12-2","title":"John 12:2","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":12,"verseStart":2,"chapterEnd":12,"verseEnd":2},"content":"<p>they made. So 4:16; 5:1; Lu 5:29; 14:12; Re 3:20 Martha. Mt 26:6; Mr 14:3; Lu 10:38-42; 12:37; 22:27 Lazarus. 9,10; 11:43,44</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-12-3","title":"John 12:3","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":12,"verseStart":3,"chapterEnd":12,"verseEnd":3},"content":"<p>took. 11:2,28,32; Mt 26:6,7-13; Mr 14:3-9 Lu 10:38,39 ointment. Ps 132:2; So 1:12; 4:10,13,14 spikenard.Spikenard is a highly a romatic plant growing in India, whence was made a very valuable unguent or perfume, used at the ancient baths and feasts. It is identified by Sir W. Jones with the {sumbul} of the Persians and Arabs, and {jatamansi} of the Hindoos; and he considers it a species of the valerian, of the triandria monogynia class of plants. The root is from three to twelve inches long, fibrous, sending up above the earth between thirty and forty ears or spikes, from which it has its name; stem, lower part perennial, upper part herbaceous, sub-erect, simple, from six to twelve inches long; leaves entire, smooth, fourfold, the inner radical pair petioled and cordate, the rest sessile and lanceolate; pericarp, a single seed crowned with a pappus.</p>\n<p>anointed. Mr 14:3; Lu 7:37,38,46 filled. So 1:3</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-12-4","title":"John 12:4","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":12,"verseStart":4,"chapterEnd":12,"verseEnd":4},"content":"<p>one. 1Sa 17:28,29; Ec 4:4 Judas Iscariot. 6:70,71; 13:2,26; 18:2-5; Mt 10:4; Lu 6:16</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-12-5","title":"John 12:5","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":12,"verseStart":5,"chapterEnd":12,"verseEnd":5},"content":"<p>was. Ex 5:8,17; Am 8:5; Mal 1:10-13; Mt 26:8; Mr 14:4; Lu 6:41 three hundred. 6:7; Mt 20:2; *marg: Mr 14:5 and given. Mt 26:9; Lu 12:33; 18:22</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-12-6","title":"John 12:6","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":12,"verseStart":6,"chapterEnd":12,"verseEnd":6},"content":"<p>not. 10:13; Ps 14:1; Pr 29:7; Eze 33:31; Ga 2:10; Jas 2:2,6 because. 10:8-10; 2Ki 5:20-27; Ps 50:16-20; Mt 21:13; 1Co 6:10 the bag. 13:29; 2Ki 12:14,15; Ezr 8:24-34; 2Co 8:19-21; 1Th 5:22</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-12-7","title":"John 12:7","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":12,"verseStart":7,"chapterEnd":12,"verseEnd":7},"content":"<p>Let. Ps 109:31; Zec 3:2; Mt 26:10; Mr 14:6 against. 19:38-42; Mt 26:12; 27:57-60; Mr 15:42-47; Lu 23:50</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-12-8","title":"John 12:8","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":12,"verseStart":8,"chapterEnd":12,"verseEnd":8},"content":"<p>the poor. De 15:11; Mt 26:11; Mr 14:7 but. 35; 8:21; 13:33; 16:5-7; So 5:6; Ac 1:9-11</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-12-9","title":"John 12:9","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":12,"verseStart":9,"chapterEnd":12,"verseEnd":9},"content":"<p>11:43-45; Ac 3:10,11; 4:14</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-12-10","title":"John 12:10","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":12,"verseStart":10,"chapterEnd":12,"verseEnd":10},"content":"<p>11:47-53,57; Ge 4:4-10; Ex 10:3; Job 15:25,26; 40:8,9; Ec